Soho has returned to socially distant  normality a few drinks and a bite to eat  in the summer sunshine but last night on  an evening called Super Saturday  thousands spilled out onto the streets  and it was chaos  the owner of GA why Jeremy Joseph says  despite the best efforts of businesses  in the area the way the restrictions  were lifted was a disaster it was awful  I mean the whole situation was  completely wrong  this is not what venues wanted there are  two pictures of yesterday there's old  Compton Street which was a complete mess  out of control and people forgot there  was a pandemic and then there were  inside venues where I mean you can have  a look what it's like in there at the  moment  you know we've got screens in between  tables where a reduced capacity it's  completely safe  the sheer number of people out and in  close proximity is a worrying sign many  will be wondering how this fits with  keeping the country safe throughout this  pandemic the government has said it is  guided by the science but the truth is  the easing of the lockdown is really a  grand experiment nobody knows what  effect it will have on the infection  rate and it's clear that in some parts  of the country like here for instance  last night alcohol in quantity is a poor  mixer with social distancing the  government's own scientific advisers  have said the reopening is not a  risk-free next step and those  responsible for policing the streets say  the dangers are obvious the alcohol and  social distancing clearly is going to be  a difficulty and the government needs to  accept that they can't just have their  head in the sand and say everything was  fine there were some some difficulties  with trying to manage our social  distancing and when you put alcohol in  the mix that was that was predictable  but in many parts of the country people  did appear to socially distance  responsibly as they enjoyed some of the  old freedoms the health secretary is  warning tougher measures may be  reintroduced if the rules are flout it  we don't shirk from bringing in more  drastic measures if that's what's needed  to control the virus but the overall  plan has always been through this lip  ding of the lockdown to try to lift the  national measures but also be absolutely  crystal clear that will tackle local  flare-ups when we see them this weekend  wasn't just about the hospitality sector  people have also been allowed to gather  in places of worship for the first time  in months the look and I think is about  three months now or they're about that  we've not been here so it was exciting  is to know that the government did when  the pastor said we are going to do this  we have our procedures in place and  we're ready so yeah overall the  government is claiming the biggest  easing of restrictions so far was a  success the reality is though it won't  be for a couple of weeks until we see  the true cost in terms of infections of  returning to a kind of normal Alex Rossi  Sky News central London
 

